From afa6a3c7fedbbf164b6543931adb3e5476f02537 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sasha Zero <sasha@ripostelabs.xyz>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 08:44:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Add H.264 CAVLC mvd hooks to ffedit

Export and re-apply every mvd syntax element of P and B macroblocks
(all partition and sub-partition shapes) as the "mv" (final vector,
pred + mvd) and "mv_delta" (raw mvd) features. CAVLC, progressive
frames only.

The MPEG-4 path mirrors every bit read into a PutBitContext. That
does not fit H.264, where the decoder works on an RBSP with the
emulation prevention bytes stripped and the CAVLC residual reader
uses raw cache macros. Instead, ffe_h264_mvd() wraps the se(v) reads
in h264_cavlc.c and records the bit range and replacement value of
each edited mvd. After the slice is decoded, ffe_h264_nal_done()
rebuilds the NAL: unchanged bits are copied, the recorded ranges are
re-encoded as se(v), rbsp_trailing_bits are regenerated and the
emulation prevention bytes are re-inserted. NALs without edits and
everything between NALs are copied verbatim, so an unedited round
trip is bit-exact.

Frame lifecycle follows the MPEG-4 hooks: JSON contexts are attached
to the picture's AVFrame at frame start and finalised in
ff_h264_field_end(). CABAC, field/MBAFF pictures and length-prefixed
(AVCC) input are refused with a clear message when an mv feature is
requested; plain copies of such files still work.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
